EXT. SNOWFIELD BELOW A CRASHED CARGO PLANE - DAY

A white slope. A twisted tail section jutting from the drift. Wind drags snow in long ropes.

MARISOL ARROYO-LINDGREN (40s, parka, bleeding scalp under a knit cap) kneels beside the wreck, digging with a tin plate. ANTON BELLWEATHER-ODUYA (50s, lanky, one arm tucked inside his jacket) stands over her, stamping his feet.

She digs. He watches.

ANTON
Anything?

MARISOL
Not yet.

He paces. She keeps digging. The plate scrapes on metal. She stops.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
There's a hatch.

ANTON
Open it.

She grips the edge. Pulls. It doesn't give. He kneels beside her with his good hand. They pull together. It gives a quarter inch and stops.

MARISOL
It's frozen.

ANTON
Of course it's frozen. Everything's frozen.

She sits back on her heels. He stares at the hatch. Wind howls.

MARISOL
(muttering)
Don't panic. Don't panic. Don't panic.

ANTON
I'm not panicking.

MARISOL
I wasn't talking to you.

He looks at her. She keeps her eyes on the hatch.

He takes off his glove with his teeth. Puts a bare palm on the metal. Holds it there. Winces. Pulls away.

ANTON
It's warm underneath.

MARISOL
What?

ANTON
Something's warm. Something's running in there.

She puts her hand on it. Holds it. Her eyes widen.

MARISOL
A generator.

ANTON
Or an engine.

She slams the plate against the hatch handle. Once. Twice. A crack of ice. On the third blow the handle snaps loose and the hatch swings up on a gust of warm, oily air.

They both recoil. Then they lean in together and stare down a ladder into a hold lit by a single red emergency lamp.

ANTON (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Thank God.

MARISOL
(to Anton)
Don't thank anyone yet.

She swings her legs over the edge. He grabs her sleeve.

ANTON
Wait. We should tie off.

MARISOL
With what?

He looks around at the white. Nothing. She pulls free.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
(to the whole slope, as if calling the wind to witness)
We're going down!

She goes. He hesitates. Then follows, sliding the hatch shut over his head. The snow closes in behind them.

INT. CARGO HOLD - CONTINUOUS

Crates strapped to the floor. A red lamp swinging gently. The hold smells of diesel and spilled coffee.

She reaches the bottom first. He lands heavily, groans, and clutches his arm.

She hands him a flashlight from her belt. He plays the beam across the crates. Stenciled words: MEDICAL SUPPLIES.

ANTON
Medical.

MARISOL
That's useful.

ANTON
Is it?

MARISOL
Bandages. Antibiotics. Whatever's in there for your arm.

He looks at his arm. He's been avoiding it. He pulls out the sleeve and sees the dark soak in the fabric.

ANTON
(quietly)
It's worse than I said.

MARISOL
I know.

ANTON
You knew?

MARISOL
You've been holding it like a baby bird for three hours.

He laughs, once, and sits down on a crate. She pries the lid off the next one with the tin plate. It's full of foil blankets.

She wraps one around his shoulders. Sits beside him with another.

They don't speak. The red lamp swings.

ANTON
(finally)
Marisol.

MARISOL
Mm.

ANTON
If it comes to it. If one of us has to walk out.

MARISOL
Don't.

ANTON
If it comes to it, it should be you.

She doesn't answer. He turns his head, looking for her face in the red light. She is staring at the hatch, jaw set, hands wrapped tight in the blanket.

MARISOL
(low, to the hatch)
It won't come to it.

The wind outside rises, a long hollow note against the hull. She does not look away from the ladder. He tucks the blanket tighter and does the same.
